# outline_collection

Retrieves one existing Outline collection's metadata. Configure exactly one of `id` or `name`. Both attributes are optional and computed, so the lookup also returns the attribute you did not configure.

Use an unrestricted API key owned by an active workspace admin. The provider verifies the caller before lookup. The data source targets Outline 1.10.1 only.

## Lookup behavior

ID lookup requires a canonical lowercase, nonzero UUID. A collection's short URL ID, name, or full URL is not accepted as `id`.

Name lookup compares exact, case-sensitive names across every page. Duplicate collection names are valid in Outline. No exact match or multiple exact matches produce an error; use a UUID to disambiguate. Active and archived collections participate in the same lookup, so an archived collection with the same name can make a name ambiguous.

The lookup includes private and archived collections in the admin's workspace, but excludes trash. Name lookup sends `includeListOnly = true` and an explicit empty `statusFilter` array, which includes both active and archived collections in Outline 1.10.1. The provider validates every page, duplicate UUIDs, and consistent totals rather than accepting an incomplete list.

Workspace admins can read private collection metadata without membership. This does not grant access to private documents. The data source does not manage documents, collection grants, or the collection itself.

A missing lookup returns an error, not an empty result. Only Outline's verified `collections.info` `404 not_found` proves UUID absence. A `403` never proves absence, including for a UUID missing from the workspace list. Inaccessible cross-workspace collections, failed admin checks, unexpected route or proxy 404 responses, and malformed pages remain errors.

## Example usage

{{ tffile "examples/data-sources/outline_collection/data-source.tf" }}

Replace the example UUID with an existing collection's ID. Each lookup returns `id`, `name`, `description`, `permission`, and `sharing`.

`permission` is the default workspace access, not a summary of direct or group grants. It can be `read`, `read_write`, the server's `admin` value, or null for private. Unlike the resource, the lookup accepts archived collections and the `admin` default.

`description` is the current Markdown landing-page description. A null server description becomes an empty string. `sharing` reports whether public document sharing is allowed in the collection, subject to workspace policies; it does not report whether a public share exists.

